ough these ideas, Foucault treats language as a dynamic field shaped by power relations, where meaning arises within discourse, institutional practices, and knowledge production rather than from fixed sign systems alone. His work emphasizes how discourse organizes social reality, including penal institutions, by constructing what counts as knowledge and normality. The notions of enunciation (how statements are produced), assemblages (networks of practices, actors, and material conditions that generate meaning), and the abstract machine (an abstract organizing principle for discursive systems) are central to his approach to analyzing language and meaning. Overall, Foucault’s theoretical contributions provide a framework for understanding language, power, and meaning in modern societies, with particular attention to prisons and other disciplinary structures within discourses of power.", "MICHEL FOUCAULT:Michel Foucault was a French philosopher, historian, and social theorist whose work analyzes how power circulates through political economy, psychoanalysis, social production, and discourses. He argued that capitalism not only organizes production and labor but also externalizes desire, shaping both individuals and the development of the human sciences, which he saw as arising from production and representation. In his exploration of power, discipline, and knowledge, Foucault introduced concepts such as disciplinary power, regimes of signs, and an abstract machine that regulates relations among major institutions—the prison, the school, the barracks, the hospital, and the factory—illustrating how modern power operates through structured spaces and practices. He maintained that content and expression are not reducible to signifieds or to a base-superstructure, and he analyzed how language functions within regimes of statements to shape knowledge and social order. Additionally, Michel Foucault is cited as the author of the May 1964 essay “La folie, l’absence d’œuvre,” published in La Table ronde, reflecting his early engagement with questions of madness, authorship, and critique within contemporary thought.", "GERMANY:Germany is a country mentioned in the text as a geopolitical entity referenced in the discussion of signs, bodies, and contents", "NOVEMBER 20, 1923:NOVEMBER 20, 1923 is the date associated with the referenced work \"Postulates of Linguistics.\" In the source text, this date is cited (see note 21—Trans.) as part of the reference to the work \"Postulates of Linguistics.\" It also appears as a heading in the text—\"NOVEMBER 20, 1923: POSTULATES OF LINGUISTICS\"—which anchors the subsequent discussion of linguistic postulates and the relationship between signs and contents. In summary, NOVEMBER 20, 1923 serves both as a bibliographic anchor for the postulates and as a structural heading guiding the analysis of how signs relate to contents within the linguistic framework described.", "NOVEMBER 20, 1923: POSTULATES OF LINGUISTICS:NOVEMBER 20, 1923: POSTULATES OF LINGUISTICS refers to a dated linguistic publication or document bearing the title \"Postulates of Linguistics\" issued on November 20, 1923. The work engages with linguistic theory, examining signs and ideology as part of the broader discourse on language and society. Across the provided descriptions, there are no additional entities (organizations, persons, geographic locations, or events) associated with this item beyond the publication itself. The item is consistently described as a publication or section titled \"POSTULATES OF LINGUISTICS,\" dated November 20, 1923, and is referenced within discussions of linguistic theory.", "LABOV:Labov is a linguist mentioned in the text as participating in a debate with Chomsky, contributing to the discussion on language invariants and linguistic structure", "JAMES JOYCE:James Joyce (JAMES JOYCE) is an Irish author and modernist writer, author of Ulysses. In the referenced text, he is identified as a notable figure in discussions of language variation and the distinction between major and minor languages. The text also notes that Joyce’s work is described as appealing to different linguistic procedures when compared with Roussel’s, highlighting a contrast between Joyce’s linguistic approach and that of Roussel. Taken together, these points position Joyce within broader conversations about how language operates in literary practice, emphasizing his significance in debates over language variation and stylistic procedures in modernist writing.", "EZRA POUND:American poet and critic; key figure of Modernism; mentioned in the text in relation to Ulysses and the discussion of linguistic variation.", "POSTULATES OF LINGUISTICS:Linguistic publication dated November 20, 1923; a header in the text indicating a scholarly article on linguistics.) <|COMPLETE|>", "IRS:Internal Revenue Service, the U.S. federal agency responsible for collecting taxes, processing tax returns, and enforcing tax laws as part of the internal revenue service administration.", "LEVI-STRAUSS:LEVI-STRAUSS refers to Claude Lévi-Strauss, the French anthropologist and a foundational figure in structuralism. The descriptions portray him as a prominent thinker whose work illuminates how human societies are structured. He is described as demonstrating that the dualist organization of primitive peoples can take both circular and linear forms, capable of encompassing any number of groups. He is cited as a respected observer on causality, dissymmetry, and repetition, with analysis focusing on how elements interlock through dislocation and how the sign relates to its cause. Further, Lévi-Strauss is depicted as describing signifying regimes and the role of the signifier within semiotics, situating signs within a broader system of meaning-making. Collectively, these notes present Lévi-Strauss as a central figure who analyzes structural relationships, binary oppositions, and the interlocking parts of culture, while highlighting the semiotic mechanisms—signifiers and signs—that mediate causal relations and meaning across societies.", "ROBERT LOWIE:Robert Lowie, American anthropologist who described ethnographic topics (including the Crow) and is referenced in the discussion of signs and semiotics within the text.", "STRINDBERG:A PERSON mentioned in the text; the quote \"Surveillance by the face\" is attributed to Strindberg as part of the discussion on faciality and the signifying regime.", "OEDIPUS:OEDIPUS, the legendary king of Thebes, emerges across the descriptions as both a concrete mythic figure and a central analytic symbol. In myth, he is noted for torturing himself by gouging out his eyes and, after learning the truth, departing into exile—a narrative that demonstrates the consequences of knowledge, fate, and self-punishment. In psychoanalytic and philosophical discourse, Oedipus is invoked as a foundational symbol to illuminate the interplay of sexuality, desire, and the social/familial structures analyzed in Freudian theory, serving as an origin point for explanatory schemes about social formation and familial reproduction. The figure is repeatedly used as a universal limit and a displaced representative in discussions of the socius and desire, providing a framework for understanding how collective and individual investments of desire are organized and constrained.\n\nOedipus is also described as a metaphorical instrument through which the State’s imposition of particular artistic and scientific forms can be critiqued within schizoanalytic discourse, with symbolic figures like Oedipus framing political and psychoanalytic critique. He functions as an index of reactionary social investment, with image-models—such as mother, fiancée, mistress, wife, saint or whore, princess, and maid—depended upon him within the sociohistorical field and its investments of desire. The text situates Oedipus within mythic discourse as the “Oedipus’ chorus” and within psychoanalytic theory as a universal figure in discussions of the Oedipal dynamic, illustrating how imagined structures sustain subjugated groups and shape desire.\n\nMoreover, Oedipus is described as receiving a sign “from too far away and from too close,” a trope used to demonstrate how signs function to shape learning and action through repetition, thereby illustrating how repetitive processes can reproduce or intensify patterns of transgression within both psychoanalytic and schizoanalytic contexts. In sum, OEDIPUS is a central, multifaceted figure who embodies mythic tragedy, psychoanalytic theory, and political critique, anchoring discussions of desire, social organization, and the symbolic machinery that governs human behavior.", "CLERAMBAULT:A 20th-century psychiatrist who formulated the schema of passional delusion (erotomania) and described the postulate as the point of subjectification, along with the relation between the subject of enunciation and the subject of the statement; his theory is cited in the text as the framework for understanding erotomania and related delusions.)<|COMPLETE|>(\"entity\""], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_4817e4c46dbc7407c8064b41f24be72c1627e2e06aab8fe810da84818ad88648_v2"}